<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Learned About the History of Panama. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Learning to Research History</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> I Learned About the History of Panama.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: justify;" colspan="3">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write short, simple essays with basic structure on familiar topics. Interactive: Provide feedback on peers' essays, including suggested edits.</p>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a short essay summarizing a historical event and reflecting on its impact (e.g., "Panama separated from Colombia in 1903, which allowed the country to shape its national identity and control its own policies."). Can edit a peer's essay for clarity and organization (e.g., "Try starting with the most important event!"). Can provide feedback on a classmate's essay about Panama's history (e.g., "You might add more details about the date!").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Warm-up:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays 3-4 images of significant Panamanian historical events (e.g., separation from Colombia, construction of the Panama Canal, Torrijos-Carter Treaties signing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are asked to quickly jot down 2-3 keywords or short phrases that come to mind for each image, reflecting on what they *learned* in previous lessons. This activates prior knowledge of key *events* and *vocabulary* such as *independence*, *canal*, *construction*, *transformation*.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Modeling:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models writing a concise sentence describing one of the events using the past simple and relevant vocabulary. Example: "Panama *declared* its *independence* from Colombia in 1903."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then models how to incorporate a simple piece of reported speech related to the event: "Historians *reported* that this *separation* was a crucial turning point for the nation's *identity*."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation focus: Teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of -ed endings for past tense verbs like /d/ in "declared" and /t/ in "shaped" while writing.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we writing about events that are happening now or events from the *past*?" (Expected answer: Events from the past.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we *report* what someone said, do we use quotation marks or a reporting verb like 'said' or 'reported'?" (Expected answer: A reporting verb.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning to Research History".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Material:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A short, factual excerpt (approx. 150-200 words) from a *historical report* or *documentary transcript* about a specific Panamanian *historical event* (e.g., the *construction* of the Panama Canal, its *significance*, and *transformation* of the *economy*). The text will integrate *past simple*, *reported speech*, and *passive voice*.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Competence:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students can understand key information and the main idea of short written texts on familiar historical topics.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the excerpt silently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main *event* described in the text?" "What was the most important *impact* mentioned?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match key dates (e.g., 1903, 1914) from the text with the *historical events* that *occurred* on those dates.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points out examples of *past simple*, *reported speech* (e.g., "Historians *say* the canal *transformed* Panama's *economy*"), and *passive voice* (e.g., "The canal *was completed* in 1914.") within the text, highlighting their function in historical writing.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Using *past simple*, *reported speech*, and *passive voice* to write sentences about *historical events*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Target vocabulary: *history*, *Panama*, *independence*, *separation*, *canal*, *significance*, *construction*, *economy*, *events*, *identity*, *declared*, *learned*, *shaped*, *reported*, *completed*, *transformed*, *historical*, *relevant*, *significant*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pragmatic function: *Asking about important historical events* and *sharing opinions*.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Gap-fill:</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences about Panamanian *history* that have gaps for verbs in the *past simple*, *passive voice*, or *reporting verbs*. They complete the sentences using the correct form of the verbs provided in parentheses and relevant vocabulary from a word bank.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "Panama ____ (declare) *independence* in 1903." (declared) "The *canal* ____ (complete) in 1914." (was completed) "Journalists ____ (report) that the *transformation* of the *economy* was immediate." (reported)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Sentence Transformation:</b> Students are given sentences in active voice or direct speech about *historical events*. They rewrite them using the *passive voice* or *reported speech*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: "The U.S. built the *canal*." → "The *canal* *was built* by the U.S." "A politician said, 'This *event* will *shape* our *identity*.'" → "A politician *said* that this *event* would *shape* their *identity*." Teacher reminds students to be careful with verb tenses and pronoun changes in reported speech.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Collaborative Sentence Writing:</b> In pairs, students receive a short prompt (e.g., "The *significance* of the *separation* of *Panama*") and brainstorm *events* and details related to it. They then collaboratively write 3-4 sentences summarizing key facts and impacts, ensuring they use at least one instance of *past simple*, *reported speech*, and *passive voice*. They also practice *asking about important historical events* to clarify details with their partner (e.g., "When did Panama separate from Colombia?" or "What impact did the construction of the Panama Canal have on the country's economy?").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning to Research History".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Task: Essay Writing – "Reflecting on a Historical Moment"</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students will individually write a short essay (approximately 80-100 words, 2-3 paragraphs) summarizing a chosen *historical event* from *Panama*'s past (e.g., *Panama's separation* from Colombia, the *construction* of the *Panama Canal*, the transfer of the *canal*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The essay must:
                        <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Briefly introduce the *event* using *past simple*.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Include 1-2 key facts, potentially using *passive voice* for description.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Reflect on its *impact* or *significance* for *Panama*'s *identity* or *economy*, using *reported speech* to include a perspective (e.g., "Historians *say* this *event* *shaped* the nation's *legacy*.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Utilize at least 5-7 *relevant* vocabulary words from the theme.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to *reflect* on the *transformation* or *legacy* of the *event*. They can *compare* or *describe* elements of *Panama*'s *culture* or *economy* before and after the *event*.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer assessment using a checklist and teacher observation.</p>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Assessment Task:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students exchange their essays with a partner. They use a provided checklist to review their peer's essay for clarity, organization, and the correct use of target grammar (*past simple*, *reported speech*, *passive voice*) and *vocabulary*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Partners provide constructive feedback and suggest edits using phrases like: "Try starting with the most important *event*!", "You might add more details about the date!", "Is this verb in the *past simple*?", "Can you use *passive voice* here?", or "I think you could add more *relevant* vocabulary here." This engages them in *sharing opinions* and *asking for clarification on historical facts*.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Organization:</b> Is the essay easy to understand? Does it have a basic structure?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Accurate use of *past simple*, *reported speech*, and *passive voice*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Integration of *relevant* nouns, verbs, and adjectives related to the theme.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Completeness:</b> Does it summarize an *event* and reflect on its *impact*?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing you *learned* about writing a *historical report* today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did giving feedback to your classmate help you improve your own writing?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing a short *essay* about *history* did you find most challenging, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about using *past simple*, *reported speech*, and *passive voice* to *describe historical events* in writing?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students successfully engaged with the *writing* task, demonstrating a good grasp of *past simple* and *relevant vocabulary*. There were some common errors in reported speech (tense shifting) and proper use of the passive voice. The peer feedback activity encouraged critical thinking and interactive learning.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For the next lesson, more explicit scaffolding will be needed for the transformation rules of reported speech, especially when integrating it into longer sentences. I will also provide sentence starters for reflections to help students articulate the *impact* and *significance* more clearly.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will take their written historical summaries and use them as a basis to explain and interpret historical information to a wider audience, possibly through a presentation or infographic, applying their newly *gained* *writing* and *research* skills.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>